GR-DOS: MEM /HUMAN shows the memory sizes as df -h does (issue #89, HELP MEM).

/HUMAN, in any case, prints the summary's sizes (total, available,
largest executable program, and the EMS and extended memory lines) in
binary units as gr-dos/df16's df -h does: whole bytes below 1 KiB
("0B", "512B"), otherwise KiB, MiB or GiB with one decimal, rounded up,
moving up a unit at 1024.0. They are new messages 52 to 58, the
summary's texts without "bytes", with the size right-aligned in the
same 10 columns. /HUMAN may come with /PROGRAM or /DEBUG, in either
order; a second /HUMAN, or both /PROGRAM and /DEBUG, is "Too many
parameters", as before. Without /HUMAN the output is unchanged. The
lists of /PROGRAM and /DEBUG stay in hex.

Applied to a copy of v4.0/src before it is converted to CRLF (Makefile,
PATCHES). `make check` builds without it.

